        <bioghist encodinganalog="3.2.2">
          <p>The KDW bronze objects depicted in this series were created by what was originally known as the silversmithing workshop in the early 1960s, and later became known as the precious metals department. Bronze appears to have been a less popular metal in the early decades of the KDW, with only one item, a bronze platter with a red patina, created circa 1965. The other 13 items were designed and manufactured circa 1981 to 1984, which correlates to a similar diversification of materials in the manufacture of jewellery at this time, possibly influenced by the steep rise in silver and gold prices in the early 1980s.
            Ten items depicted in this series are recorded with unidentified designers, although two of these items can be linked to Oísin Kelly and Richard Eckersley from records in the silver holloware series. These are the PA/Irish Times Management Trophy in the style of a seagull in flight which was designed by Oísin Kelly and is recorded in this series as file BZ/02, and a spiral tower shaped trophy commissioned by the Home Studies Institute which was designed by Richard Eckersley and is recorded in this series as BZ/12. The other items with unidentified designers include: the bronze platter with a red patina; an award for Lisdowney Community Centre Greyhound Meeting, Thurles 1981 and a GAA trophy in a similar design with the inscription 'Duais Coiste Contae Cill Channaigh 1983'; two golf trophies, one for Noonan Construction Golf Outing 1982 which included a golf ball embedded in a clear acrylic or glass panel, and the second was awarded for the 'Irish Nationwide Journalists Interprovencials, Kilkenny 1984'; a similar shaped trophy was presented to a retired member of An Garda Síochána, Tramore in 1985; a plaque was produced to honour the generosity of Edward O'Sheehy of Danesfort who helped in the restoration of an abbey; a three piece cruet set; a trophy featuring a motif of a wheelchair on top of a marble base; and two round metal boxes featuring cut-out motifs from their lids – one featuring a shamrock and the second featuring a decorative floral pattern  – this particular box is also depicted in the silver holloware series as a presentation piece for the official opening of Fieldcrest Ltd. 25 September 1980.
            Oísin Kelly designed another two items depicted in this series, both sculptural in form. The first bronze trophy depicts two hurling players battling for control of the ball. Some photographs related to this file depict maquettes of the sculpture made prior to its production. The second file depicts a bronze trophy of two footballers (possibly Gaelic) stretching and leaping for a ball in the air. Photographs belonging to this file also illustrate alternative poses for a footballer. One photo in this file appears to be unrelated to the football theme, and depicts a sculpture of a stocky, naked male figure with his arms elevated over his head holding a hammer preparing to strike.
            Tony O'Hanlon, a designer from the graphic design department was recorded as the designer of a plaque in this series, most likely he was responsible for the typographic layout. The bilingual plaque was dedicated to Brother Michael O'Clerigh of the Franciscan Order and Chief of the Four Masters of Louvain College.
            Finally, Des Byrne was responsible for the design of a trophy presented to Michael O'Hare by the GAA, which was a bronze replica of an old fashioned radio microphone.</p>
        </bioghist>
        <scopecontent encodinganalog="3.3.1">
          <p>The 14 files in this series document the bronze objects designed and produced by the KDW precious metals department over the period from 1965 to 1984. The documentation is in the form of slides, photographs and negatives. The items depicted in this collection include: a platter, a cruet set, round boxes, trophies, sculptures and plaques.</p>
        </scopecontent>
